Clonal mast cell expansion without organ dysfunction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-047
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of rapid-onset widespread rash, intense bone pain, and risk of severe systemic anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden development of intense full-body hives, localized bone aching, and high anxiety regarding impending airway closure based on prior history. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out evolving anaphylactic shock, ensure airway patency, and provide acute systemic mast cell stabilization prior to regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My skin is covered in intensely itchy red-brown spots, my thighs ache deeply, and I'm terrified my throat will close.
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of mast cell disorders presents with a severe acute degranulation event. The flare was triggered by minor physical friction 2 hours ago. Symptoms include the rapid eruption of intensely pruritic skin lesions urticaria pigmentosa, profound systemic flushing, and deep, throbbing bone pain particularly in the femurs. The patient reports a history of recurrent anaphylaxis and is experiencing extreme hypervigilance. Denies current wheezing, lip swelling, or vomiting.
Functional Status: Ambulating but severely distressed by pruritus and bone pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; extreme psychological distress regarding the unpredictable nature of the anaphylactic risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 104 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Flushed, diaphoretic, pacing, frequently scratching torso. No audible stridor.
Physical Exam: Airway is widely patent; no oropharyngeal edema. Lungs clear bilaterally. Dermatological exam reveals widespread reddish-brown maculopapular lesions that hive and intensely swell when stroked Darier's sign positive. Deep palpation of the long bones elicits moderate ache. Hemodynamics are remarkably stable despite systemic flushing, consistent with clonal mast cell expansion without organ dysfunction.
Environmental Observations: EpiPens visible and readily accessible on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Took one OTC antihistamine earlier with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Systemic Indolent Mastocytosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Anaphylactic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Stable blood pressure without hypotensive collapse, patent airway without wheeze/stridor, and lack of systemic GI involvement.

Acute Viral Exanthem - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Classical positive Darier's sign (lesions swelling upon friction) and chronicity of the underlying urticaria pigmentosa.

Bone Metastases - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is diffuse, tied strictly to the acute mast cell degranulation timeline, and typical for bone marrow mast cell infiltrate rather than destructive focal lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of explosive systemic flushing and prior history of recurrent anaphylaxis mandated an immediate, face-to-face home assessment to clinically exclude impending airway obstruction or cardiovascular collapse. Having confirmed stable hemodynamics and a purely cutaneous/skeletal flare, emergency transport was avoided. However, the severity of the pruritus and bone pain required immediate parenteral administration of antihistamines to chemically halt the mast cell degranulation cascade and relieve profound distress overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Promethazine 25mg IM to rapidly block H1 receptors and provide mild sedation. Prescribed high-dose oral Famotidine 20mg twice daily for H2 blockade. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, Vitamin C, and targeted H1/H2 therapies. Advised strict avoidance of NSAIDs which can trigger degranulation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By continuously applying a cold, damp towel to the most intensely itching areas for 15 minutes, you will physically constrict the superficial blood vessels, directly preventing further histamine release into the skin and rewarding yourself with immediate soothing relief.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Checked expiry dates on auto-injectable adrenaline (EpiPens valid).
Follow-up: Routine GP and Immunology review in 3 days to optimize daily prophylactic mast cell stabilizers.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops lip/tongue swelling, stridor, severe wheezing, sudden vomiting, or feelings of faintness/dizziness when standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Indolent mastocytosis carries a lifelong risk of unpredictable anaphylaxis; constant vigilance and immediate access to adrenaline are mandatory.
Clinical Course: The acute flushing and intense itching should significantly fade within 2-4 hours post-injection. The bone pain may take 24 hours to fully recede.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Use your EpiPen and call 000 immediately if your tongue swells, your voice changes, or you suddenly struggle to pull air into your lungs.

